    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d372">

                <pb facs="0623" n="609" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg609"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European War 1939/2688: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-06-20T17:00:00+03:00">June 20, 1944—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:02 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2197. Department’s 1461, June 9, 10 a.m. In my talk with Vyshinski<anchor n="72"
                        xml:id="d372-f1"/> yesterday I informed him that although the President’s
                    views on <pb facs="0624" n="610" xml:id="pg610"/> the subject of unconditional
                    surrender remained unchanged the President had approved omitting the use of the
                    term in connection with the satellite countries by the propaganda services and
                    giving the services a degree of latitude in this connection. Vyshinski remarked
                    that this step was in accord with the British attitude. I said that I had
                    mentioned this question in view of the coming participation of the Soviet
                    representative on the London Propaganda Committee.<anchor n="73"
                        xml:id="d372-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Repeated London as 120.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d372-f1" n="72" xml:id="d372-f1-content">
                <p>Andrey Yanuaryevich Vyshinsky, Assistant People’s Commissar for Foreign Affairs
                    of the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d372-f2" n="73" xml:id="d372-f2-content">
                <p>Known as the London Coordinating Committee for Political Warfare; see <ref
                        target="#pg1455">pp. 1455</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
